export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","addon":"energy-code","name":"Energy code check","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"energy-code-start","scope":"addon:energy-code","title":"Energy code check: getting started","summary":"Check a house against California's 2025 Energy Code prescriptive package: the climate zone from the ZIP code, wall U-factors from the code's own tables, ceiling and floor insulation, and window U-factor, SHGC and area. A guide, not a compliance document.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["energy code","Title 24","Part 6","California","climate zone","ZIP code","prescriptive","component package","U-factor","R-value","SHGC","fenestration","west-facing","insulation","CF1R","performance","LSC","compliance","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"where-the-numbers-come-from","text":"Where the numbers come from","level":2},{"id":"what-the-model-gives-and-what-you-state","text":"What the model gives, and what you state","level":2},{"id":"a-project-file-for-the-approved-program","text":"A project file for the approved program","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-do","text":"What it does not do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESwitch the add-on on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E, then open the \u003Cstrong\u003EEnergy code check\u003C/strong\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EPlace and size\u003C/strong\u003E, type the site&#039;s ZIP code. The climate zone appears. Check the conditioned floor area: it comes from the model&#039;s outline, and you can type your own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EOutside walls\u003C/strong\u003E, each exterior wall type has a card. Choose the stud size, the spacing, the insulation between the studs and any continuous insulation. The U-factor is read from the code&#039;s own table and shown under the boxes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ECeiling or roof\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EFloor\u003C/strong\u003E, choose what the house has and its insulation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EWindows and doors\u003C/strong\u003E, type the rated U-factor and SHGC from the window labels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the \u003Cstrong\u003EResult\u003C/strong\u003E. A tick meets the code&#039;s figure, a cross does not, and a question mark could not be checked, with the reason beside it. Each row names its table or section.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-numbers-come-from\"\u003EWhere the numbers come from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe code&#039;s numbers are in a code pack, made by script from the California Energy Commission&#039;s published documents: Table 150.1-A, Section 150.0 and Reference Joint Appendix JA4 of the 2025 Building Energy Efficiency Standards, and the Commission&#039;s list of climate zones by ZIP code. The pack holds numbers and section numbers, never the code&#039;s text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-model-gives-and-what-you-state\"\u003EWhat the model gives, and what you state\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrom the model:\u003C/strong\u003E exterior wall types and their areas, windows and doors in exterior walls, each window&#039;s compass direction, the floor area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrom you:\u003C/strong\u003E what is between the studs, continuous insulation, ceiling and floor insulation, the radiant barrier, the windows&#039; rated values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat you state is kept in the drawing, so it travels with the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-project-file-for-the-approved-program\"\u003EA project file for the approved program\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EThe performance path\u003C/strong\u003E, press \u003Cstrong\u003ESave the project file (.ribd25)\u003C/strong\u003E. It saves the envelope in the format of CBECC, the Energy Commission&#039;s own compliance program, so the energy consultant starts from your model. The panel lists what the file assumed (roof pitch, roofing, floor framing) and what the consultant must add (heating, cooling, water heating, solar panels, the garage). The button stays off while something the envelope needs is missing, and the panel says what.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file is checked against the program&#039;s published data model. We have not opened it in the program itself: treat it as a starting file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-do\"\u003EWhat it does not do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt does not produce or register a certificate of compliance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt checks the envelope only. Heating, cooling, water heating, ventilation and solar panels are listed as not checked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt does not do the performance approach. That needs software the Energy Commission has approved. The project file gives that software a start; it is not a result.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Switch the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons, then open the Energy code check tab. Under Place and size, type the site's ZIP code. The climate zone appears. Check the conditioned floor area: it comes from the model's outline, and you can type your own. Under Outside walls, each exterior wall type has a card. Choose the stud size, the spacing, the insulation between the studs and any continuous insulation. The U-factor is read from the code's own table and shown under the boxes. Under Ceiling or roof and Floor, choose what the house has and its insulation. Under Windows and doors, type the rated U-factor and SHGC from the window labels. Read the Result. A tick meets the code's figure, a cross does not, and a question mark could not be checked, with the reason beside it. Each row names its table or section. Where the numbers come from The code's numbers are in a code pack, made by script from the California Energy Commission's published documents: Table 150.1-A, Section 150.0 and Reference Joint Appendix JA4 of the 2025 Building Energy Efficiency Standards, and the Commission's list of climate zones by ZIP code. The pack holds numbers and section numbers, never the code's text. What the model gives, and what you state From the model: exterior wall types and their areas, windows and doors in exterior walls, each window's compass direction, the floor area. From you: what is between the studs, continuous insulation, ceiling and floor insulation, the radiant barrier, the windows' rated values. What you state is kept in the drawing, so it travels with the file. A project file for the approved program Under The performance path, press Save the project file (.ribd25). It saves the envelope in the format of CBECC, the Energy Commission's own compliance program, so the energy consultant starts from your model. The panel lists what the file assumed (roof pitch, roofing, floor framing) and what the consultant must add (heating, cooling, water heating, solar panels, the garage). The button stays off while something the envelope needs is missing, and the panel says what. The file is checked against the program's published data model. We have not opened it in the program itself: treat it as a starting file. What it does not do It does not produce or register a certificate of compliance. It checks the envelope only. Heating, cooling, water heating, ventilation and solar panels are listed as not checked. It does not do the performance approach. That needs software the Energy Commission has approved. The project file gives that software a start; it is not a result."},{"slug":"energy-code-what-is-data","scope":"addon:energy-code","title":"California's building code as data: what we can and cannot hold","summary":"What a regulation pack holds (numbers, units, clause numbers, a link back) and what it never holds (the code's text), what is in the California packs, and where a number was read on a secondary copy.","section":"start","order":2,"keywords":["Title 24","California Building Standards Code","regulation pack","Regulations","guard height","42 in","California Electrical Code","CALGreen","wildland-urban interface","copyright","citation","clause","source","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-a-regulation-pack-holds","text":"What a regulation pack holds","level":2},{"id":"what-it-never-holds","text":"What it never holds","level":2},{"id":"what-the-california-packs-hold","text":"What the California packs hold","level":2},{"id":"read-the-source-line","text":"Read the source line","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-a-regulation-pack-holds\"\u003EWhat a regulation pack holds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pack holds \u003Cstrong\u003Enumbers, units, clause numbers and short labels written by us\u003C/strong\u003E, and says where each number was read. Nothing else. The Regulations add-on checks your model against those numbers and shows the clause beside every result, so you can look the rule up in the code itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-never-holds\"\u003EWhat it never holds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe code&#039;s text.\u003C/strong\u003E California&#039;s building standards are thirteen parts (Title 24). They are published by the International Code Council, the National Fire Protection Association and IAPMO under their own copyright claims, and are free to read only on their own viewers. We list the parts and link to those viewers; we do not copy, summarise or host the text, and the add-on is never a copy of the code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA result that counts as compliance.\u003C/strong\u003E The 2025 Energy Code has no Energy Design Rating. Its performance path works in source energy and long-term system cost and must be run in software the Energy Commission has approved. The Energy code check does the prescriptive package and the mandatory minimums only, and says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELocal amendments.\u003C/strong\u003E A city or county can amend the code. The California pack is the state&#039;s.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-california-packs-hold\"\u003EWhat the California packs hold\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnergy:\u003C/strong\u003E Table 150.1-A, the mandatory minimums and the assembly U-factor tables of the 2025 Energy Code, read by script from the Commission&#039;s own documents (see \u003Cem\u003EEnergy code check: getting started\u003C/em\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe Regulations add-on&#039;s California pack:\u003C/strong\u003E the guard height, \u003Cstrong\u003E42 in.\u003C/strong\u003E (CRC R321.1.2; the model code says 36 in.), which a drawn guard is now checked against; the electrical rules under the \u003Cstrong\u003ECalifornia Electrical Code\u003C/strong\u003E&#039;s clauses (210.52 and 210.70), not the model code&#039;s chapter 39, which California does not use; and cited numbers from the residential code&#039;s California-only sections, CALGreen and the wildland-urban interface code. The numbers the checker does not test yet are listed with their clause, so they are there to read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA reference list\u003C/strong\u003E of the thirteen parts with their publishers, dates and links.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-source-line\"\u003ERead the source line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach rule&#039;s note says whether its number was \u003Cstrong\u003Eread on an official page\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003Eread on a secondary copy\u003C/strong\u003E. The official viewers block automated reading, so most residential-code numbers were read on a full-text copy and the amendment status on the official table. A number read on a secondary copy is a guide: confirm it on the publisher&#039;s page, whose link is in the note. The packs have not been reviewed by a California professional. The registered professional and the local authority decide.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What a regulation pack holds A pack holds numbers, units, clause numbers and short labels written by us, and says where each number was read. Nothing else. The Regulations add-on checks your model against those numbers and shows the clause beside every result, so you can look the rule up in the code itself. What it never holds The code's text. California's building standards are thirteen parts (Title 24). They are published by the International Code Council, the National Fire Protection Association and IAPMO under their own copyright claims, and are free to read only on their own viewers. We list the parts and link to those viewers; we do not copy, summarise or host the text, and the add-on is never a copy of the code. A result that counts as compliance. The 2025 Energy Code has no Energy Design Rating. Its performance path works in source energy and long-term system cost and must be run in software the Energy Commission has approved. The Energy code check does the prescriptive package and the mandatory minimums only, and says so. Local amendments. A city or county can amend the code. The California pack is the state's. What the California packs hold Energy: Table 150.1-A, the mandatory minimums and the assembly U-factor tables of the 2025 Energy Code, read by script from the Commission's own documents (see Energy code check: getting started). The Regulations add-on's California pack: the guard height, 42 in. (CRC R321.1.2; the model code says 36 in.), which a drawn guard is now checked against; the electrical rules under the California Electrical Code's clauses (210.52 and 210.70), not the model code's chapter 39, which California does not use; and cited numbers from the residential code's California-only sections, CALGreen and the wildland-urban interface code. The numbers the checker does not test yet are listed with their clause, so they are there to read. A reference list of the thirteen parts with their publishers, dates and links. Read the source line Each rule's note says whether its number was read on an official page or read on a secondary copy. The official viewers block automated reading, so most residential-code numbers were read on a full-text copy and the amendment status on the official table. A number read on a secondary copy is a guide: confirm it on the publisher's page, whose link is in the note. The packs have not been reviewed by a California professional. The registered professional and the local authority decide."}]};
